chunk_findings.detail:
The refusal predicate `_unknown_phase` is documented in this same diff to return `""` when the argument *is an item* (`"""..."" when it is an item.`), i.e. an existing item id is explicitly treated as a valid `--phase` value ("as next does"). But `board` cannot slice by an item id: the very next lines are

    for ph in sorted(st.phases(), key=lambda p: (p.priority, p.id)):
        if phase and ph.id != phase:
            continue

so an item id never equals any `ph.id`, `phases` stays empty, and `board` falls through to the normal path printing "_No phases yet_" with exit 0 — the exact silent-empty-board symptom that Bc2acd426f4 is supposed to eliminate. Concretely, with an item id `i1` that lives in phase `P` (and is therefore accepted by `_unknown_phase`), `ddflow board --phase i1` returns `""` from `_unknown_phase`, skips the new `O.failed` branch, matches no phase, and prints "_No phases yet_" / exits 0. `next --phase i1` on the same input slices to the item, so the change's claim that board now refuses unknowns "as `next` does" does not hold for the item-id class the shared predicate deliberately lets through. Board needs a phase-id-aware validity check (or resolution of an item to its phase) rather than `_unknown_phase`.
